{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"A\":\"Develop a serial structural candidate: normalize orientation so the squared boundary-pair dimension is min(M,N), materialize/layout data so the scanned dimension is contiguous, and compare rolling strip sums against the current row-prefix approach. Initially retain a correct frequency-map method to isolate the structural gain. Preserve all rectangular cases, negative entries, and 64-bit answer accumulation; validate against the oracle.\",\"B\":\"Develop an OpenMP candidate that partitions independent boundary-pair work across the 4 ARM cores, with strictly private prefix/frequency state and a 64-bit reduction. Start from a correct pair-enumeration implementation, avoid shared unordered_map state and false sharing, and compare schedules/thread counts only through measured runs. Check that thread-local memory remains reasonable and results are deterministic.\",\"C\":\"Investigate exact replacement of unordered_map overhead in the inner scan. Prefix sums are bounded by scanned_dimension*1000 (at most 1,000,000), so benchmark a bounded direct-frequency table with per-pair timestamps or touched-entry reset, including guarded out-of-range queries for s-k; compare against a compact/custom hash alternative if dense per-thread storage loses. Also assess buffered input and eliminate needless repeated hash lookups, but keep general valid-input semantics and 64-bit counts.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only independently correct, measured tree outputs. Re-measure each candidate and each proposed composition rather than assuming gains add; likely evaluate orientation/layout plus dense counting plus OpenMP. Audit transpose/orientation indexing, negative sums, k outside the prefix range, timestamp lifecycle, overflow, allocation footprint on 4 cores, and exact one-line output. Select the fastest valid measured integration or retain the best standalone candidate.\",\"analysis\":\"This is Problem A. Baseline is valid at 0.25273 s on 4-core ARM64 and performs O(M*N^2) work, always squaring columns, with a long-long unordered_map in every column-pair scan. The principal general opportunities are orientation symmetry for rectangular matrices, locality-friendly rolling strip accumulation, removal of hash allocation/probing via the proven bounded prefix-sum domain, and safe pair-level parallelism. The answer can reach well beyond 32 bits, while individual strip prefixes fit signed int; no approach may depend on particular test values or replace the computation.\"}"}}
